chromium-embedded-framework for Debian
======================================

This package uses Multiple Upstream Tarballs (MUT) format with 3.0 (quilt)
source format. All source components are distributed as separate tarballs.

================================================================================
SOURCE PACKAGE STRUCTURE (MUT)
================================================================================

The source package consists of 3 tarballs:

    chromium-embedded-framework_VERSION.orig.tar.xz
        CEF source code (main upstream tarball)

    chromium-embedded-framework_VERSION.orig-chromium.tar.xz
        Chromium browser source (vanilla, from Debian chromium package)

    chromium-embedded-framework_VERSION.orig-chromium-debian.tar.xz
        Debian Chromium patches (from Debian chromium source package)

When extracted with dpkg-source -x, these create:

    chromium-embedded-framework-VERSION/
    ├── cef/                    <- CEF source (from orig.tar.xz)
    ├── chromium/               <- From orig-chromium.tar.xz
    ├── chromium-debian/        <- From orig-chromium-debian.tar.xz
    └── debian/                 <- Packaging files

NOTE: No depot_tools dependency. Like Debian Chromium, this package calls
system /usr/bin/gn and /usr/bin/ninja directly (from generate-ninja and
ninja-build packages).

================================================================================
BUILD INSTRUCTIONS
================================================================================

Standard Build (from source package):

    $ dpkg-source -x chromium-embedded-framework_*.dsc
    $ cd chromium-embedded-framework-*/
    $ dpkg-buildpackage -us -uc

The MUT components are automatically extracted by dpkg-source -x. This is the
recommended build method as it correctly handles component directory naming.

Build Time: ~2-7 hours (depends on hardware; 2h on modern 24-core, 7h+ on older systems)
Disk Space: ~40 GB required

Development Workflow (from git checkout):

When working from a git checkout (not a dpkg-source extracted package), you
must first create and extract the source package:

    $ cd /path/to/chromium-embedded-framework
    $ dpkg-source -b .                    # Create .dsc and debian.tar.xz
    $ cd ..
    $ dpkg-source -x chromium-embedded-framework_*.dsc build-dir
    $ cd build-dir
    $ dpkg-buildpackage -us -uc

This is necessary because MUT component tarballs (orig-chromium.tar.xz)
have internal directory names that differ from the component names.
dpkg-source -x handles the renaming automatically.

DO NOT attempt to manually extract component tarballs - use dpkg-source -x.

Obtaining Source Tarballs:

Once the package is in the Debian archive, use:

    $ apt-get source chromium-embedded-framework

Or download with dget:

    $ dget https://deb.debian.org/debian/pool/.../chromium-embedded-framework_VERSION.dsc

================================================================================
FOR DEBIAN DEVELOPERS REVIEWING THIS PACKAGE
================================================================================

Quick Start (copy-paste these commands):

    # 1. Clone the repository
    git clone https://salsa.debian.org/mendezr/chromium-embedded-framework.git
    cd chromium-embedded-framework

    # 2. Find the Chromium version available in Debian
    apt-cache policy chromium | grep Candidate
    # Example output: Candidate: 147.0.7727.116-2

    # 3. Download MUT tarballs (adapt versions to match your release)
    #
    #    Parameters:
    #      147.0.3+ds3       = CEF package version with +ds suffix (from debian/changelog)
    #      7727              = CEF upstream branch (from cef/CHROMIUM_BUILD_COMPATIBILITY.txt)
    #      147.0.7727.116-1~deb13u1 = Chromium Debian version (from step 2)
    #
    ./debian/scripts/get-orig-tarballs.sh 147.0.3+ds3 7727 147.0.7727.116-1~deb13u1

    # 4. Create source package
    dpkg-source -b .

    # 5. Extract and build
    cd ..
    dpkg-source -x chromium-embedded-framework_*.dsc build-dir
    cd build-dir
    dpkg-buildpackage -us -uc

Notes:
- Step 3: The script downloads CEF, Chromium, and Debian Chromium
  patches automatically. It creates all 3 MUT component tarballs. It tries
  apt-get source first, then falls back to dget from security.debian.org or
  deb.debian.org if needed.
- Step 5: dpkg-source -x applies quilt patches and extracts all MUT components.
  The build is fully self-contained - no external files needed.
- Build requires ~40GB disk space and 2-7 hours depending on hardware.

================================================================================
COMPONENT TARBALLS
================================================================================

1. CEF Source (orig.tar.xz)
---------------------------
Obtained from: https://bitbucket.org/chromiumembedded/cef
Branch: Must match Chromium's build number (e.g., branch 7727 for Chromium 147.x)

2. Chromium Source (orig-chromium.tar.xz)
-----------------------------------------
Obtained from: Debian chromium package source (vanilla upstream tarball)

IMPORTANT: You must download the correct Chromium version that matches CEF's
requirements. Check cef/CHROMIUM_BUILD_COMPATIBILITY.txt for the required version.
For CEF branch 7727, this is Chromium 147.0.7727.x.

Command (with specific version):
    apt-get source --download-only chromium=147.0.7727.116-1~deb13u1

To find available versions:
    apt-cache policy chromium

This tarball contains vanilla Chromium source, identical to what Debian chromium
distributes as chromium_*.orig.tar.xz. The get-orig-tarballs.sh script renames
it to the MUT component name automatically.

3. Debian Chromium Patches (orig-chromium-debian.tar.xz)
--------------------------------------------------------
Obtained from: Debian chromium source package (chromium_*.debian.tar.xz)

Contains the Debian Chromium packaging directory (patches/, rules, etc.).
These patches are applied during Phase 1 (setup-source) of debian/rules to integrate
Chromium with Debian system libraries (libc++, system FFmpeg, build fixes, etc.).

The get-orig-tarballs.sh script creates this MUT component automatically from
the chromium_*.debian.tar.xz downloaded alongside the Chromium source.

Why a separate MUT component (not embedded in orig-chromium.tar.xz):
- Keeps vanilla Chromium source verifiable (byte-identical to Debian chromium orig)
- Makes Debian patch application transparent and auditable
- Makes the source package fully self-contained (works in sbuild/buildd)

4. Rust Standard Library (system rust-src)
------------------------------------------
The build uses system rust-src package (Build-Depends: rust-src).

Debian rust-src 1.91.1+ provides all files required by Chromium's build system,
including sync/nonpoison/condvar.rs and rwlock.rs that were missing in earlier
versions. No bundled Rust tarball is needed.

================================================================================
DESIGN DECISIONS
================================================================================

1. WHY FULL CHROMIUM SOURCE (not just headers)
----------------------------------------------
CEF uses internal Chromium APIs, not just the public API. For example:

- CEF includes 43+ headers from content/browser/ (internal implementation)
  rather than content/public/browser/ (stable public API)
- libcef/browser/browser_host_base.cc includes internal headers like
  content/browser/renderer_host/render_frame_host_impl.h
- CEF's off-screen rendering extends content::RenderWidgetHostViewBase
  from content/browser/renderer_host/render_widget_host_view_base.h

These internal classes don't have stable ABIs and their implementation can
change between Chromium versions. CEF hooks into Chromium's internal browser
infrastructure, requiring source-level integration with a matching Chromium
version. A headers-only approach (chromium-headers package) would not work
because the ninja build targets depend on compiled Chromium object files.

2. NO DEPOT_TOOLS DEPENDENCY
-----------------------------
Unlike upstream CEF builds that use Google's depot_tools (gclient, autoninja),
this package calls system /usr/bin/gn and /usr/bin/ninja directly -- the same
approach used by the Debian chromium package.

depot_tools is purely an orchestration layer: no GN files, ninja rules, or
built binaries reference it. The build uses:
- generate-ninja package (/usr/bin/gn) for build file generation
- ninja-build package (/usr/bin/ninja) for compilation
- CEF's version_manager.py and make_distrib.py called directly

NETWORK ISOLATION: This package does NOT access the internet during build.
All source is bundled in the MUT tarballs.

3. LOCAL GIT INITIALIZATION
---------------------------
debian/rules creates local git repositories with git init && git commit.
This satisfies CEF's version_manager.py and make_distrib.py which use
git for version detection. This is completely network-free.

================================================================================
CONFIGURATION DECISIONS
================================================================================

1. RAM-BASED PARALLEL JOB LIMITING
----------------------------------
CEF/Chromium compilation requires ~3GB RAM per parallel job. The build system
automatically limits ninja parallelism based on available RAM to prevent OOM
killer from terminating the build.

Implementation in debian/rules:
- Respects DEB_BUILD_OPTIONS=parallel=N if set (never increases beyond it)
- Calculates safe jobs based on available RAM (~3GB per job)
- Uses minimum of requested and RAM-safe values
- Considers cgroup memory limits for containerized builds (buildds)

Example behavior:
| Machine         | RAM   | CPUs | DEB_BUILD_OPTIONS | Resulting Jobs |
|-----------------|-------|------|-------------------|----------------|
| Dev workstation | 64GB  | 24   | (none)            | 21 (64/3)      |
| Dev workstation | 64GB  | 24   | parallel=8        | 8              |
| Small buildd    | 16GB  | 8    | parallel=8        | 5 (16/3)       |
| Large buildd    | 128GB | 64   | parallel=64       | 42 (128/3)     |

This follows established Debian patterns:
- llvm-toolchain-19: https://sources.debian.org/src/llvm-toolchain-19/1:19.1.4-1/debian/rules/#L92
- polymake (4GB/job): https://sources.debian.org/src/polymake/4.12-3/debian/rules/?hl=15#L15
- Pending debhelper MR: https://salsa.debian.org/debian/debhelper/-/merge_requests/128

2. DISTRIBUTION CONFIGURATION (--minimal)
------------------------------------------------------
CEF's make_distrib.py creates only the minimal distribution (--minimal flag).

Why minimal-only:
- Skips doxygen documentation generation (saves 1-2GB memory)
- Minimal distribution contains everything needed for Debian packages:
  * libcef.so (Release build)
  * Headers (include/)
  * CMake configuration files
  * Resources (PAK files, locales, ICU data)
  * ANGLE libraries (libEGL.so, libGLESv2.so)

What minimal excludes (not needed for Debian packages):
- Debug build (libcef.so with debug symbols - we use dh_strip instead)
- Test applications (cefclient, ceftests)
- Doxygen documentation

The --no-format flag is also passed to skip clang-format on headers
(purely cosmetic, avoids spawning 16,000+ processes).

3. SYSTEM LIBC++ (use_custom_libcxx=false)
------------------------------------------
Uses Debian's libc++-19-dev instead of bundled libc++.
Aligns with Debian Chromium's approach.

4. SYSTEM LLVM/CLANG (clang-19)
-------------------------------
Uses Debian's clang-19 toolchain. No bundled compiler.

5. FFMPEG (statically linked)
-----------------------------
FFmpeg is statically linked into libcef.so from Chromium's third_party/ffmpeg/.
Built with proprietary_codecs=true and ffmpeg_branding="Chrome" for H.264/AAC support.

================================================================================
PATCHES
================================================================================

CEF Patches (debian/patches/*.cef.patch)
----------------------------------------
Applied via quilt during dpkg-source extraction.
Patch paths have cef/ prefix (e.g., cef/tools/gn_args.py).
See debian/patches/series for the order.

Chromium Patches (debian/patches/chromium/*.chromium.patch)
-----------------------------------------------------------
Applied manually via patch -p1 in debian/rules.
These patches modify Chromium source, not CEF source.

================================================================================
SYSTEM BINARIES
================================================================================

1. NODE.JS (system nodejs package)
----------------------------------
Chromium expects Node.js at third_party/node/linux/node-linux-x64/bin/node.
This package uses system Node.js instead of any bundled binary:

- nodejs is in Build-Depends
- debian/rules creates symlink: ln -sf /usr/bin/node .../third_party/node/.../node
- Patch 0007-skip-nodejs-version-check.chromium.patch bypasses version check
  (system node version may differ from Chromium's expected version)
- chromium/third_party/node/linux/* is in Files-Excluded (debian/copyright)

2. ROLLUP (system rollup package)
---------------------------------
Similarly, system rollup replaces bundled @rollup/wasm-node:

- rollup is in Build-Depends
- debian/rules creates symlink to /usr/share/nodejs/rollup

================================================================================
VERSION TRACKING (debian/watch)
================================================================================

The debian/watch file uses Version 5 format with the Untrackable field.

uscan cannot automatically track CEF upstream versions because the CEF
Bitbucket repository has no git tags. CEF uses only branch numbers (e.g.,
7680) which correspond to Chromium build numbers, not CEF versions. The
actual CEF version (e.g., 146.0.5) is derived from a VERSION file inside
the source tree, which uscan cannot extract from git refs.

The MUT component tarballs (chromium) could be tracked individually,
but the Untrackable field applies globally to the entire watch file.
Their stanzas are preserved as comments in debian/watch.

To obtain MUT tarballs, use the helper script:

    $ ./debian/scripts/get-orig-tarballs.sh [VERSION] [CEF_BRANCH]

Manual tracking resources:
- CEF branches: https://bitbucket.org/chromiumembedded/cef/branches/
- CEF builds: https://cef-builds.spotifycdn.com/index.html
- Chromium pool: https://deb.debian.org/debian/pool/main/c/chromium/

================================================================================
VERSIONING (+ds REPACKAGING SUFFIX)
================================================================================

This package uses the +dsN suffix (Debian Source repackaging) to track changes
in MUT component tarballs. The upstream version format is:

    CEF_VERSION+dsN

Where:
    CEF_VERSION = CEF upstream version (e.g., 146.0.5)
    N           = repackaging counter, incremented when any MUT input changes

The +dsN counter increments whenever a MUT component tarball changes content,
most commonly when Debian Chromium releases a security update. This is required
because Debian archive orig tarballs are immutable: a new tarball with different
content requires a new upstream version.

Example version progression (from CEF 146 era):

    146.0.5+ds1-1     <- initial (chromium 146.0.7680.71)
    146.0.5+ds1-2     <- packaging fix only (same tarballs)
    146.0.5+ds2-1     <- chromium security update (146.0.7680.80)
    146.0.5+ds3-1     <- another chromium security update (146.0.7680.200)
    147.0.1+ds1-1     <- new CEF version (resets +ds counter)

The upgrade-helper.sh script computes the +ds suffix automatically:
  - New CEF version: resets to +ds1
  - Same CEF, new Chromium: increments +ds
  - No input change: no version change

The changelog documents which Chromium version each +ds release was built
against, providing full traceability.

================================================================================
MAINTENANCE
================================================================================

Helper Scripts:

Two helper scripts are provided in debian/scripts/ to simplify version updates:

  - get-orig-tarballs.sh: Downloads and creates MUT tarballs
  - upgrade-helper.sh: Updates version references in debian/ files

These scripts automate the manual steps below. See each script for usage.

Updating CEF Version:
1. Run get-orig-tarballs.sh with new version/branch (creates orig.tar.xz
   and updates cef/ in the git tree automatically)
2. Update CEF_BRANCH in debian/rules
3. Update chromium version to match CEF requirements
4. Regenerate orig-chromium.tar.xz from new Debian chromium
5. Run upgrade-helper.sh (resets +ds to 1 automatically)
6. Commit cef/ and debian/ changes to git
7. Test build and fix any patch conflicts

Updating Chromium Version (same CEF branch, new Chromium security release):

When Debian releases a new Chromium version within the same branch (e.g.,
147.0.7727.116 -> 147.0.7727.200), first run upgrade-helper.sh to bump the
+ds counter and update version references, then get-orig-tarballs.sh to
create new tarballs matching the updated version:

   # 1. Bump +ds counter and update Chromium version in debian/ files
   $ ./debian/scripts/upgrade-helper.sh 147.0.3 147.0.7727.200

   # 2. Create new MUT tarballs (version from debian/changelog: 147.0.3+ds4)
   $ ./debian/scripts/get-orig-tarballs.sh 147.0.3+ds4 7727 147.0.7727.200-1

   # 3. Build
   $ dpkg-source -b .
   $ cd ..
   $ dpkg-source -x chromium-embedded-framework_*.dsc build-dir
   $ cd build-dir
   $ dpkg-buildpackage -us -uc

Updating LLVM Version:
1. Update Build-Depends in debian/control
2. Update CLANG_MVERS in debian/rules

================================================================================
FUTURE MIGRATION (When chromium-source package exists)
================================================================================

When the Debian Chromium team provides a chromium-source binary package:

Migration Steps:
1. Remove `orig-chromium.tar.xz` and `orig-chromium-debian.tar.xz` from MUT tarballs
2. Add `Build-Depends: chromium-source` to debian/control
3. Modify Step 5 in debian/rules to copy from /usr/src/chromium/ (or wherever
   the chromium-source package installs the source)
4. Adjust Step 5.5 based on the source package format (see below)

Handling Different Source Formats:
---------------------------------

The chromium-source package may provide source in two formats:

A) VANILLA SOURCE (pristine upstream):
   - Keep Step 5.5 to apply Debian Chromium patches during build
   - Extract patches from the chromium-source package
   - This is identical to our current approach

B) PRE-PATCHED SOURCE (Debian patches already applied):
   - Remove or disable Step 5.5 (patches already in source)
   - Update this documentation to note patches are pre-applied
   - Verify all expected patches are included

Detection: Check the chromium-source package documentation or test by:
   grep -l "libcxx" /usr/src/chromium/build/config/BUILDCONFIG.gn
   - If found: source is VANILLA (needs Step 5.5)
   - If not found: source is PRE-PATCHED (skip Step 5.5)

Why Migration is Straightforward:
---------------------------------
- Our Step 5.5 patch infrastructure handles both scenarios
- All Debian Chromium patches are applied visibly during build
- debian/rules already handles system library integration
- No hidden modifications in our tarballs

